    <h1 style="color:#0f1f3d;">Truck Batteries for the Middle East Market</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      The Middle East is a core JIS market — Japanese and Korean heavy trucks dominate, running
      JIS N150/N200 batteries — with European vehicles calling for DIN88/DIN100 alongside them.
      DINWEYS stocks both, with hot-climate construction and GCC-ready documentation.
    </p>

    <div style="margin:20px 0;padding:16px 20px;background:#f4f7fb;border-left:4px solid #f5a623;border-radius:6px;">
      <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin:0 0 10px;font-size:1.15rem;">Direct Answer</h2>
      <p style="margin:0;">The Middle East runs a Japanese and Korean heavy-truck base (Hino, Isuzu, Mitsubishi Fuso, UD Trucks) using JIS N150 (145G51) and N200 (190H52), alongside a premium European segment (Mercedes-Benz, Volvo, Scania, MAN) that in Gulf fitment references is also commonly specified in the N150/N200 group, with DIN88 (58827) and DIN100 (60038) as the European-code alternative. Heat is the dominant stress — summer temperatures of 35&ndash;45&deg;C accelerate lead-acid ageing — and imports clear through the GCC G-Mark under the Gulf LVE regulation, with Jebel Ali, Jeddah and Dammam as the main entry points.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin-top:32px;">Market Overview</h2>
    <p>
      The GCC heavy commercial vehicle market was valued at roughly <strong>USD 17.17&nbsp;billion (2024)</strong>,
      projected to reach about USD 23.49&nbsp;billion by 2030 at a 5.3% CAGR
      (<a href="https://www.researchandmarkets.com/report/middle-east-heavy-commercial-vehicle-market" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ResearchAndMarkets</a>).
      IMARC Group's estimate puts the market at about USD 19.2&nbsp;billion in 2026, growing at a 4.7%
      CAGR toward 2034
      (<a href="https://www.imarcgroup.com/gcc-heavy-commercial-vehicle-market" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IMARC Group</a>).
      The GCC truck market is import-reliant: established global heavyweights (Mercedes-Benz, Volvo,
      Scania, MAN) lead the premium heavy-duty segment, while Isuzu, Hino and Fuso hold strong positions
      in medium and light duty
      (<a href="https://www.indexbox.io/store/gcc-trucks-market-analysis-forecast-size-trends-and-insights/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IndexBox</a>).
      Frost &amp; Sullivan lists Daimler, Mitsubishi Fuso, Hino, Isuzu, Scania and SinoTruk among the
      major players, with Saudi Arabia the dominant GCC market
      (<a href="https://store.frost.com/commercial-vehicle-cv-market-gulf-cooperation-council-2024-2028.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Frost &amp; Sullivan</a>).
    </p>
    <p>
      The precise installed-fleet split by brand is not published in a single official source, so
      treat the mix above as structure rather than exact share. <em>Precise brand share: Not publicly verified.</em>
    </p>

    <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin-top:32px;">Climate &amp; Duty Demands</h2>
    <p>
      Heat is the defining stress across the Gulf. The region has a hot desert climate, with summer
      averages typically running <strong>35&ndash;45&deg;C</strong> and peaks beyond 45&deg;C in the interior
      (<a href="https://www.worlddata.info/asia/arab-emirates/climate.php" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WorldData — UAE climate</a>;
      <a href="https://www.climatestotravel.com/climate/united-arab-emirates" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Climates to Travel</a>).
      For a lead-acid starting battery this is harder duty than cold. Elevated temperature accelerates
      plate ageing and increases electrolyte water loss in flooded designs, which shortens service life
      and raises self-discharge while a vehicle sits.
    </p>
    <p>
      The practical consequence for buyers is the same across the GCC: thick-plate flooded construction
      with disciplined electrolyte care, or a sealed AGM option where a fleet prefers maintenance-free
      operation. Coastal hubs add humidity and salt air, which promote terminal corrosion — see the
      <a href="/markets/uae/" style="color:#1e5aa8;">UAE</a> page for the humidity detail.
    </p>

    <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin-top:32px;">Vehicle Mix &amp; Common Battery Groups</h2>
    <p>
      The battery group follows the vehicle. Japanese trucks — Hino 500/700, Isuzu F/Giga, Mitsubishi
      Fuso Fighter/Super Great and UD Quon — are specified in JIS <strong>N150</strong> (and N200 where
      heavier cranking or a taller case is required). European trucks (Mercedes-Benz Actros/Atego,
      Volvo FH/FM, Scania R, MAN TGS/TGX, DAF XF, Iveco Stralis) are commonly listed in the N150/N200
      group in Middle East fitment references, with DIN88/DIN100 as the alternative European code. The
      groups that dominate GCC fleets:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Battery group</th><th>DINWEYS model</th><th>Capacity / CCA</th><th>Typical vehicle class</th></tr>
      </thead>
