      Group-to-vehicle mapping is a third-party fitment reference (Club Assist / Century selection
      guides) — a compatible reference, not a DINWEYS OEM confirmation. Confirm dimensions, terminal
      layout and voltage against the specific truck before ordering.
    </p>

    <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin-top:32px;">Standards</h2>
    <p>
      Two standards run side by side. JIS N-series batteries (N150/N200) serve the Japanese and Korean
      truck base — and, in the Gulf, most European trucks are also specified in the N150/N200 group by
      independent fitment references. DIN/EN batteries in codes DIN88 and DIN100 cover European vehicles
      where the original fitment uses that code. Match the battery to the vehicle's group, dimensions
      and terminal orientation rather than to the region alone. For a technical comparison, see
      <a href="/compare/jis-vs-din-vs-bci/" style="color:#1e5aa8;">JIS vs DIN vs BCI</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin-top:32px;">DINWEYS Relevance</h2>
    <p>
      DINWEYS — manufactured by Chengguang Power Tech Co., Ltd. — carries the two group families that
      dominate Gulf fleets in one line: JIS N150 (145G51) and N200 (190H52) for the Japanese truck base
      and the European trucks specified in the N150/N200 group, plus DIN88 (58827) and DIN100 (60038)
      for European vehicles using the DIN code. These are positioned as <strong>compatible references</strong>
      matched by group, dimensions and CCA — not as OEM replacements — so a distributor can cover the
      region's mixed fleet from a single manufacturer and shipment.
    </p>

    <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin-top:32px;">Buyer Considerations</h2>
    <ol class="takeaways-list">
      <li><strong>Match the battery to the vehicle.</strong> Confirm group size, physical dimensions, terminal orientation and system voltage (many GCC heavy trucks are 24V — two 12V batteries in series) against the specific unit.</li>
      <li><strong>Budget for heat-shortened life.</strong> High ambient temperatures reduce service life, so a thick-plate flooded design or sealed AGM and a disciplined electrolyte/test schedule are worth more in the Gulf than in temperate markets.</li>
      <li><strong>Clear the G-Mark before shipment.</strong> GCC member states require the Gulf Conformity Mark under the LVE regulation (50&ndash;1000V AC / 75&ndash;1500V DC), with country-level schemes on top — SASO/SABER in Saudi Arabia, ECAS/EQM in the UAE. Confirm the current scheme and labelling with your importer.</li>
      <li><strong>Plan the port.</strong> Jebel Ali (Dubai) is the region's largest container hub and re-export gateway; Jeddah Islamic Port and King Abdulaziz Port Dammam serve Saudi Arabia. Choose the entry point nearest your distribution centre.</li>
    </ol>

